{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790701271,"nonce":"/A8/kGDHJi2VIIEJT5uKqyl0iqs=","title":"Python list specialization stops at known method calls","message":"On ff51664 with an LLVM-enabled release build, crypto_pyaes keeps the AES block parameter as DynamicBox through encrypt_block, sub_bytes, shift_rows, mix_columns, and their inverse methods. The sub_bytes HIR calls zb_any_getitem_i64 and zb_any_setitem_i64 in every iteration even though every visible caller passes array('B') and the S-box is already Array(B).\n\nlist_sites follows a list passed to a module function by name, but treats the same list passed to a method on a statically known class as kept. unboxed_key also consults each callee's joined list facts; a method whose joined parameter is Object reports Writes(Object), even when inference against the proposed Array(B) signature proves that all writes are integers accepted by that array.\n\nFollow non-overridden methods on known receivers and validate list writes transitively against the proposed per-signature type. Recursive call graphs use a visiting set, and an unknown call, escape, unresolved receiver, override, incompatible write, or keyword call keeps the parameter dynamic.\n\nThe resulting program creates Array(B) instances for all eight AES methods. In the 50-trial speed harness, crypto_pyaes changes from 208.18 ms mean / 112.22 ms minimum to 29.10 ms mean / 11.22 ms minimum. The changed run is 1.95x faster than its PyPy run. The dedicated lowering test verifies that a typed array flowing through a known method chain reaches both method instances without dynamic getitem/setitem calls. All 50 conformance groups pass in normal and warm-up modes.","files":null}]}